WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.094
A storm is coming toward the shore. Dark clouds fill the horizon.

2
00:00:05.095 --> 00:00:08.529
At first it seems to be a large but ordinary storm.

3
00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:11.434
But the rain falls more heavily than usual.

4
00:00:11.435 --> 00:00:18.936
The wind begins to blow faster and faster, more than one hundred kilometers per hour.

5
00:00:18.937 --> 00:00:25.687
Soon the wind shakes the street signs back and forth, as if an invisible giant were trying to pull them out of the Earth.

6
00:00:25.688 --> 00:00:30.501
Trees begin to fall. The force of the wind rips roofs from buildings.

7
00:00:30.502 --> 00:00:33.817
The wind has pushed the ocean waves farther inland.

8
00:00:33.818 --> 00:00:42.108
Suddenly the ocean seems to rise upㅡas if the sea level were suddenly six meters higher.

9
00:00:42.109 --> 00:00:46.125
The water surges forward and washes away everything that is not secure.

10
00:00:46.126 --> 00:00:54.232
This is a hurricane: a storm that can cover an area 800 kilometers wide and whose clouds can tower 16 kilometers high.

11
00:00:54.233 --> 00:01:01.903
Also known as cyclones and typhoons, hurricanes are the largest and most damaging storms on Earth.

12
00:01:01.904 --> 00:01:07.281
The word "hurricane" comes from Hurakan, the name of the Caribbean god of storms. These

13
00:01:07.282 --> 00:01:11.071
giant storms develop in different tropical regions. The

14
00:01:11.072 --> 00:01:15.385
storms from the Caribbean and the Gulf of Mexico are called hurricanes. In

15
00:01:15.386 --> 00:01:21.689
India, they are called cyclones, and in the Pacific, they are typhoons. Hurricanes

16
00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:27.991
form in an unusual way. They do not begin as a single storm that slowly grows. Near

17
00:01:27.992 --> 00:01:31.870
the equator, large thunderstorms tend to rotate. This

18
00:01:31.871 --> 00:01:39.206
movement is caused by the Earth’s rotation and the regular direction of the Earth’s winds near the equator. If

19
00:01:39.207 --> 00:01:45.997
several tropical thunderstorms are close together, they can merge to form a single giant storm called a hurricane. Hurricanes

20
00:01:45.998 --> 00:01:51.572
always rotate. Some rotate clockwise, some counterclockwise. Hurricanes

21
00:01:51.573 --> 00:02:01.363
that rotate clockwise are formed south of the equator; hurricanes that rotate counterclockwise are formed north of the equator. At

22
00:02:01.364 --> 00:02:09.747
first glance, a hurricane may appear to be just a gigantic, swirling storm cloud, but hurricanes have a complicated structure. A

23
00:02:09.748 --> 00:02:14.653
hurricane’s winds rotate faster and faster the closer they get to its center. But

24
00:02:14.654 --> 00:02:20.474
right in the middle is the eye of the hurricane, where there is almost no wind, clouds, or rain. The

25
00:02:20.475 --> 00:02:25.553
eye of a hurricane can be up to fifty kilometers wide. Immediately

26
00:02:25.554 --> 00:02:30.869
surrounding the eye of a hurricane is a towering wall of cloud called the eyewall. Here,

27
00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:38.717
the hurricane’s wind speed is fastest, so fast that it can produce tornadoes with winds of almost five hundred kilometers per hour. Hurricanes

28
00:02:38.718 --> 00:02:45.631
are formed over areas of warm ocean and are fed by the humid air above the water. As

29
00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:50.805
long as the hurricane stays over the warm ocean water, its strength increases. The

30
00:02:50.806 --> 00:02:56.134
warm air rises, generating wind and pulling in more humid air to fuel its storm. When

31
00:02:56.135 --> 00:03:00.359
a hurricane moves over land or cool water, it begins to weaken. But

32
00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:07.075
hurricanes are so strong that it can take up to ten days for one to lose its power. The

33
00:03:07.076 --> 00:03:11.777
World Meteorological Organization is responsible for naming hurricanes. Each

34
00:03:11.778 --> 00:03:17.600
year hurricanes are named in alphabetical order, alternating between masculine and feminine names. Not

35
00:03:17.601 --> 00:03:21.737
all the letters of the alphabet are used to begin the name of a hurricane. Q ,

36
00:03:21.738 --> 00:03:29.033
U , X , Y , and Z are omitted, leaving only 21 letters of the

37
00:03:29.034 --> 00:03:34.816
alphabet available. If more than 21 hurricanes form in one year, the letters of the Greek alphabet

38
00:03:34.817 --> 00:03:40.654
are used. The twenty-second hurricane would be named Alpha, the twenty-third would be named Beta, and so

39
00:03:40.655 --> 00:03:43.171
on. When a hurricane has caused a terrible

40
00:03:43.172 --> 00:03:50.760
tragedy, a country can request that the name of the hurricane be retired so that no future hurricane will have that name

41
00:03:50.761 --> 00:03:56.702
again. Just as storms can differ in the speed of their winds and the amount of rain they cause, so can

42
00:03:56.703 --> 00:04:02.063
hurricanes. Hurricanes are rated from categories one to five, category five being the

43
00:04:02.064 --> 00:04:07.290
strongest. The system of classifying hurricanes is called the Saffir-Simpson hurricane

44
00:04:07.291 --> 00:04:13.603
scale. Even a category one or category two hurricane can have a disastrous

45
00:04:13.604 --> 00:04:19.421
impact. A slow-moving hurricane can drop a tremendous amount of rain, causing landslides or

46
00:04:19.422 --> 00:04:25.112
floods. But in general, the higher the category number, the more dangerous the hurricane is to property and

47
00:04:25.113 --> 00:04:29.854
people. The high winds of a hurricane can push the surface of the ocean into a tall

48
00:04:29.855 --> 00:04:35.564
wave. When this powerful wave hits the shore, it is called the storm

49
00:04:35.565 --> 00:04:41.332
surge. Wind speed and the level of the storm surge are the primary characteristics used to classify

50
00:04:41.333 --> 00:04:50.530
hurricanes. Hurricanes are dangerous in many ways. People can be killed or injured by the flying debris propelled by their powerful

51
00:04:50.531 --> 00:04:58.065
winds. After the hurricane has passed, many may die from disease, since sanitation systems frequently break down during a

52
00:04:58.066 --> 00:05:02.565
hurricane. But 90 percent of the victims of hurricanes are drowned by the storm

53
00:05:02.566 --> 00:05:09.891
surge. Some hurricanes have left 150 kilometers of coastal land submerged underneath 3 meters of

54
00:05:09.892 --> 00:05:13.888
water. Many parts of the world have been repeatedly struck by

55
00:05:13.889 --> 00:05:19.572
hurricanes. With millions of people living in big coastal cities, large populations are at

56
00:05:19.573 --> 00:05:25.397
risk. In response to the threat of hurricanes, governments in these areas have developed evacuation

57
00:05:25.398 --> 00:05:32.237
plans. Unfortunately meteorologists cannot perfectly forecast where and when a hurricane will hit land.

58
00:05:32.238 --> 00:05:40.174
Because it is hard to evacuate whole cities with only a few days’ notice, many governments provide hurricane shelters in vulnerable areas.

59
00:05:40.175 --> 00:05:43.965
Sometimes these are large public buildings, such as stadiums.

60
00:05:43.966 --> 00:05:50.124
Other shelters are specifically designed to be used only during hurricanes.

61
00:05:50.125 --> 00:05:57.934
A powerful hurricane can leave chaos in its wake; bridges can be torn down, electricity and sanitation systems can be destroyed.

62
00:05:57.935 --> 00:06:04.645
It is recommended that people who live in areas affected by hurricanes store enough food and drinking water to last several days.

63
00:06:04.646 --> 00:06:07.321
The food should be canned and nonperishable.

64
00:06:07.322 --> 00:06:13.717
It may be a long time before help can arrive, so it is important to be prepared.

65
00:06:13.718 --> 00:06:18.824
Global warming may make coastal regions even more vulnerable to damage by hurricanes.

66
00:06:18.825 --> 00:06:22.859
Hurricanes gain their strength from warm water in tropical regions.

67
00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:26.887
If global warming increases, so will the temperature of the oceans.

68
00:06:26.888 --> 00:06:38.855
Higher temperatures, it is believed, will lead to more hurricanes than beforeㅡand these hurricanes are likely to be more powerful.

